#: ../../source/docs/troubleshooting.rst:5 ca9c6581ee19494a8f13de2333d84bcb
msgid "Archived: Troubleshooting"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/troubleshooting.rst:9 7ee51f69faa142e8b641bf47a1923488
msgid ""
"This page has not been reviewed by the wiki approver team. It appears to "
"contain a lot of out of date and unverified material - June 2015."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/troubleshooting.rst:14 dd4f474c24f745aea6edf0486091bba4
msgid "I can't get the motors to run and/or can't arm the ESCs"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/troubleshooting.rst:16 bdb70c4532ed47e99a6734cec4ac9e6c
msgid "This can be due to a number of mistakes:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/troubleshooting.rst:18 cadc83ef1c574aebbd71a6de92d4aea1
msgid ""
"Have you completed the mandatory hardware configuration steps described "
":ref:`here <configuring-hardware>`?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/troubleshooting.rst:21 d708a674aa6244039d37af58b0581ea5
msgid ""
"Are you in Acro, Stabilize or Simple mode? As a safety measure, the "
"motors won't arm in other modes."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/troubleshooting.rst:23 044ccd7d6d92472aa19515a9eb3cf89a
msgid ""
"Are you using the APM 2.5 Power Module to power the APM board, as "
"recommended? If not, and you're using one of your ESCs via the PDB to "
"power the board, make sure the JP1 jumper is on."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/troubleshooting.rst:26 e289c987c245481388e0bc5b67d28eac
msgid ""
"Are you sure you're arming them right? When the motors are armed, the arm"
" LED (green on APM1, yellow on APM 2) on the IMU will turn solid after "
"the IMU calibration flashing (about 5 seconds). Review the \\ "
":ref:`arming guide <arming_the_motors>` to make sure you're doing it "
"right."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/troubleshooting.rst:31 04d1417e08ed4abfa539e32f1d424a70
msgid ""
"Are your Yaw or Throttle channels reversed on your transmitter? Try "
"moving the Yaw stick to the far left, instead of the right. If that works"
" (green LED tuns solid after the IMU calibration, your Yaw is reversed. "
"If not, try the same with the Throttle channel: push it to max and try "
"full right yaw from there. If that works, your Throttle channel is "
"reversed."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/troubleshooting.rst:37 600fd0ea1d0f42eeb7ed8988a6627f15
msgid ""
"Is your RC gear in Mode 2? If not, you'll need to move the right stick "
"(yaw) to the right and the left stick (throttle) to the top. (Or just "
"switch back to Mode 2)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/troubleshooting.rst:40
#: ../../source/docs/troubleshooting.rst:430 779e6ecc7a3047ab9677bf67946b72b0
#: b92484acbb084e90ac431b835ec69c31
msgid ""
"Some ESCs are thrown off by some startup signals in Copter 2.x. Try "
"plugging in a USB cable first, to boot the board, and then plug in your "
"LiPo after it's already up, or just ensure that the four-wire cable from "
"your APM board to your power distribution board is disconnected during "
"regular startup. See details `here "
"<https://diydrones.com/profiles/blogs/apm2-esc-startup-problem-and-"
"workaround>`__."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/troubleshooting.rst:46 8c5cbd985f1e4000b319cdfdb17ec6ae
msgid ""
"It's possible you've changed your throttle trim settings since you did "
"your initial radio calibration. Trim the throttle down a few notches and "
"try arming again."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/troubleshooting.rst:49 6f99d38902444dfb999e7747151c9506
msgid ""
"Some ESCs have a minimum low threshold before they will arm. If you need "
"to, trim down your radio throttle and redo radio calibration."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/troubleshooting.rst:51 db99241444814fe7adb63eaa35b822f3
msgid ""
"Redo your radio calibration in the Mission Planner. Make sure that the "
"throttle channel (Ch 3) is being read properly and the values are around "
"1100 (low) and 1900 (high), more or less. Remember that your LiPo must be"
" plugged in to power the RC gear."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/troubleshooting.rst:55 e915683427404c3b9c0871d3f97d9dee
msgid ""
"If you've checked the previous things, it may be that you need to "
"recalibrate your ESCs. Do it the manual way (:ref:`here <esc-"
"calibration>` to be sure)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/troubleshooting.rst:60 acb27a4af1d64dfebf45b4977d6cb2bf
msgid "My APM 2 is locking up"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/troubleshooting.rst:62 41f994b256124a26a16a53f8b26e6b50
msgid ""
"The cause is probably that the dataflash card not initializing correctly."
" To verify this is the problem, take it out and try to power your apm 2 "
"again."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/troubleshooting.rst:66 50db8e24d9d84b58a562a153e01e2f29
msgid ""
"If this is the case, there is an easy fix. Put the dataflash back in, and"
" power the board up while looking at the terminal screen. It will "
"reformat the card (this will take A WHILE). Leave it plugged in for 2 "
"min. After that you should see a \"ready to fly\" message. You only have "
"to do this once."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/troubleshooting.rst:73 1851cfbdd4c54ec4b1fa376780c259cf
msgid "Copter tilts/flips over or wobbles crazily when I try to take off."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/troubleshooting.rst:75 6e16ee71703a4294ae68690fba110685
msgid ""
"This almost always means that you've configured it or set it up wrong. "
"Check the following:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/troubleshooting.rst:78 1c9870ad08594842b73dc2cb0ba4d947
msgid ""
"Is the APM board facing forward and in the direction of forward motion "
"(towards the forward arm in + mode and between the arms in x mode)?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/troubleshooting.rst:81 ea3b31c1b03144b6a12f1e9a412c42ab
msgid "Have you set your quad orientation right in the setup process: + or x?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/troubleshooting.rst:83 be8c23970eda493ea4a185dd0a55623d
msgid ""
"Check that all the motors are hooked up right, turning the right way and "
"the pusher/puller props are on the right motors and oriented the right "
"way."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/troubleshooting.rst:86 ca4f069b27f144d6a3fdc3f66ab3b889
msgid ""
"Check the signal wires on the power distribution board to make sure they "
"are oriented correctly. If you've reversed two, it will cause Copter to "
"flip. This should be noticeable by doing the hand test as well. (It "
"should have also shown up in the Motors test above)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/troubleshooting.rst:90 c6064665ae3f47768626ef26ca187248
msgid ""
"Check that your props can't turn on their motor shafts, as if they "
"weren't tightened down enough. If one can, despite its prop being "
"tightened, that may mean that its motor shaft has been pushed down into "
"the motor too far for the prop mount to get a grip. Take off the prop and"
" and use a small allen wrench to loosen the little set screw that holds "
"the shaft onto the motor. Then with pliers pull the shaft out until it's "
"the same height as those of the other motors. Then tighten the set screw,"
" and put the prop back and tighten it up again."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/troubleshooting.rst:98 2a1120b7dfef41cf9afc4fbf8e88825b
msgid ""
"Was the quad stable and flat for the whole calibration routine at "
"startup? Was it stable and flat during the setup process? (If need be, do"
" it again)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/troubleshooting.rst:101 98f390fb9efe4b9fbe61a3216cfa4401
msgid ""
"Did you calibrate your ESCs? They should all start together when you "
"advance the throttle. If need be, do it again manually, as described "
":ref:`here <esc-calibration>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/troubleshooting.rst:104 c6886ede4cd646fab462e7eb1fe649fe
msgid ""
"Did you setup and test your radio? Run test/radio to see. Pitch, Roll, "
"Throttle and Yaw should all be 0 or near zero (60 or less). Move the "
"pitch and roll stick to the lower right, you should see approximately "
"4500, 4500 in Channels 1 and 2."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/troubleshooting.rst:108 7213302cc9674030a0b04e8f97a6e187
msgid ""
"Have you checked all your sensors in the Mission Planner to make sure the"
" hardware is working right?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/troubleshooting.rst:112 5ed9b8b2c76c4760a9014757a3bb73c5
msgid ""
"My overpowered copter rockets into the air can't be brought down unless I"
" cut the throttle"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/troubleshooting.rst:114 0ec532d477ea475a9c0cf6b595b25315
msgid ""
"Very over powered multicopters can require so little throttle to hover "
"that even the minimum throttle (13%) is enough for them climb. In "
"addition, the default Rate roll/pitch gains, which are meant for weaker "
"copter, are too high for a powerful copter and can lead to roll/pitch "
"instability. The Copter firmware may try to compensate for this "
"roll/pitch instability by increasing the overall throttle which can lead "
"to the copter climbing further (Note: in this particular case Copter "
"increasing the overall throttle is counter-productive but in a properly "
"tuned quad it helps in cases where there is a motor failure)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/troubleshooting.rst:124 e66c6fbfcca042c193e52eac8afe7b7a
msgid "The possible solutions are:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/troubleshooting.rst:126 e5bcb6c1770046bcbdd542a536b9026b
msgid ""
"add extra weight to your frame to bring the hover throttle closer to mid "
"stick"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/troubleshooting.rst:128 03bbf23897864fc28c173d6ec21a6e31
msgid ""
"reduce the THR_MIN parameter (default is 130 or 13%) to the lowest value "
"that will still keep all motors spinning reliably"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/troubleshooting.rst:130 a5bd753c16bd4c50a1033523a770f0d3
msgid ""
"set the rate roll/pitch gains low (perhaps 1/2 the default) during the "
"first flight and increase them from there. Consider using the CH6 tuning "
"feature to allow you to tune your roll/pitch gains for the first few "
"flights."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/troubleshooting.rst:138 574118e3e5a546e1bbea1c70b0acfeff
msgid ""
"My copter motors spin but the copter wont take off (or motors may not "
"spin at all)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/troubleshooting.rst:140 0fcab629923442e7b8db1e33c0b5d360
msgid ""
"While there are a few different reasons for copters to be perceived as "
"underpowered, this condition applies to motors that simply aren't "
"spinning fast enough (if at all!) to generate the lift needed, even "
"though you know they can spin faster.  Some instances discovered recently"
" showed someone had accidentally imported the wrong parameter file into "
"their copter from the 3D Robotics Aero, and when they then loaded the "
"correct parameter file, the THR_MAX parameter was left unchanged."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/troubleshooting.rst:149
#: ../../source/docs/troubleshooting.rst:165 446c1f1866e946179196e8e2fd1a539c
#: 7b012ad3e90a4e00899ec70bfbcc9aa3
msgid "Check the following:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/troubleshooting.rst:151 e28e2dca426e40c69bcbe2430068150a
msgid "Connect to the copter via your GCS"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/troubleshooting.rst:152 9ef5a8e0ffcd4d6c9848a7a4217de8c0
msgid "Go to Full Parameter List"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/troubleshooting.rst:153 064b12dafd33447783c21313a551df55
msgid ""
"Examine the THR_MAX parameter and make sure the value is between 800 and "
"1000.  The default is 1000."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/troubleshooting.rst:156 f52883e0c378412db3814783b9a684ca
msgid "FYI: The 3D Robotics Aero preset param file sets this to 80."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/troubleshooting.rst:158 b0ceb3b9851e4e8a8a21148d76edc22a
msgid ""
"If the THR_MAX parameter is less than 800 and you don't recall making "
"this change, set the parameter to 1000 and Write Params out to the copter"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/troubleshooting.rst:163 13ad5989d9e04e92bcd9176906bbe0a9
msgid "My radio setup isn't reading the RC channels right or hangs"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/troubleshooting.rst:167 ff4205e0d34d465287825e2661a5864c
msgid ""
"Click on that Calibrate Radio button. That will start the measurement "
"process."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/troubleshooting.rst:169 ab351e57eea94ea09e4cd6bb81f78257
msgid ""
"Are you sure your receiver is getting power and is paired with your "
"transmitter (it should show a solid green light). Make sure your "
"transmitter is on!"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/troubleshooting.rst:172 d2e6c72b6b7e40499a615bf09251ad68
msgid ""
"If you assembled the board yourself, double-check your soldering! A tiny "
"short on the APM board could disable all the channels."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/troubleshooting.rst:174 e0988cb54bdb4b5d83ccb64a5bc305d1
msgid ""
"We've had some reports of problems with older FM RC gear and even the "
"occasional digital receiver, for reasons that are not clear (most likely "
"low voltage output). Can you try a different receiver? This at least will"
" eliminate that possibility."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/troubleshooting.rst:178 40d1e27a65f843e58d2b6f09bab01cee
msgid ""
"We've also had reports of people having trouble with Spektrum radios, "
"which have a funky binding process. This is what works: To bind the "
"receiver and telemetry (TM1000+AR8000 to DX8) module, connect them both "
"together(TM1000 to AR8000). With the transmitter off (DX8 off), press the"
" tiny button on the side of the telemetry module (TM1000) and power the "
"receiver (plug the LiPo batteries but do not plug the 4-wire connection)."
" Both the receiver and telemetry module will start to blink (if they do "
"not blink TM1000 is no good). When that happens turn on your radio "
"holding the trainer/bind button and it will bind (stay at least 10ft "
"away). Make sure that when it binds it says something like \"binding dsmx"
" ------ receiver with telemetry\". You may have to do this twice if it "
"doesn't pick up the telemetry module the first time."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/troubleshooting.rst:191 11625e58c72943dca2586ae474f694d3
msgid ""
"Check your cables! The signal wire should be at the top, and ground at "
"the bottom."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/troubleshooting.rst:193 bdfbc87c2c7243f1986b526e5dddafcb
msgid ""
"Finally, if all else fails, try to narrow down the problem. First, check "
"that the receiver is working right by plugging a servo into the output "
"you're testing and making sure that it's outputting a good signal. Then "
"plug one known-good RC channel into the APM input channels, from 1-7, one"
" at a time and see if any are read. If some channels are read and some "
"are not, you may have a soldering error or may have fried one or more "
"inputs."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/troubleshooting.rst:200 4997b53a7b884e46884d0fd236bd8e63
msgid ""
"If you still can't diagnose the problem, contact the customer support "
"from the store you bought it from."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/troubleshooting.rst:204 7c5d8d1f09e44a5eae1168c7d9efab13
msgid ""
"My Copter isn't as stable as I'd like or doesn't Loiter/Hold "
"Altitude/Navigate reliably"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/troubleshooting.rst:206 b0733168a48f4d7e8a2624cc108ffd93
msgid ""
"Every copter is different, and even the official kit can have differences"
" in motor performance or balance depending on how you've put it together."
" So we've made it relatively easy to tweak many of the performance "
"settings to turn your copter perfectly. That said, tuning is something of"
" an art, so it helps to read a bit more before you start tweaking."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/troubleshooting.rst:213 9503fe8536604f45a0e0b979178729b0
msgid "There is a guide to PID tuning \\ :ref:`here <tuning>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/troubleshooting.rst:217 3408f423845f413bb47d5c2847eb1686
msgid "My Copter flies okay, but it tends to tilt one way or another"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/troubleshooting.rst:219 614eb056e4f54a39a2860c1ec1560503
msgid ""
"Make sure the CG (center of gravity) of the copter is dead center. Then "
"run the level command on a flat surface. (hold disarm for ~15 seconds to "
"invoke.) You can also fly in auto-trim mode in a windless (important!) "
"environment--any wind will cause the changes you make to work against you"
" when the quad rotates 180°. Hold arm for ~ 20 seconds to fly in auto-"
"trim mode for about 45 seconds. It will exit auto-trim automatically and "
"fly normally. (Don't worry about counting out the time, just wait for the"
" flashing lights to enter either mode."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/troubleshooting.rst:228 78dc06a7e1e747cf8a2416ac1ca562a3
msgid ""
"You \\ **can** use your radio pitch and roll trims, but remember to re-"
"center them when you set up your radio with the config tools. I'm not a "
"fan of trimming the radio for quads, but NEVER, NEVER trim yaw. (Quads "
"are also susceptible to drafts. They will need constant correction unless"
" you install an optical flow sensor. One day...)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/troubleshooting.rst:235 f429e24e61204a71a557eb410a7e0a5c
msgid "My Copter Moves In a Direction even though the Stick is Centered"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/troubleshooting.rst:237 026d1590b1dc49d783aae3bfd72e0806
msgid ""
"It is very important that the RC Channel 1 and 2 Trim parameter values "
"(for the Roll and Pitch axes) are equal to the PWM value displayed in "
"Mission Planner for the stick in its center position during Radio "
"Calibration."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/troubleshooting.rst:242 9269ec8dc6084b389bea96d99b93e42d
msgid ""
"To check this Start Mission Planner, connect and go to the Advanced "
"Parameter List and write down the parameter for RC Trim PWM (HS1_TRIM) "
"and RC Trim PWM (HS2_TRIM)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/troubleshooting.rst:245 2486937d4aea4fc499c5799d4b334bc7
msgid ""
"Now start up the radio Calibration function in Mission Planner and turn "
"on your RC Transmitter."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/troubleshooting.rst:247 7abc0d084cb94854ad08de7179ce1c2c
msgid ""
"With the Roll and Pitch Stick centered, the values of the channel 1 "
"(roll) and channel 2 (pitch) PWMs should be the same as the values for "
"the parameters you wrote down above."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/troubleshooting.rst:250 6ae5b92825be42e9ae7b2f038779161a
msgid "If they are not recalibrate the radio."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/troubleshooting.rst:251 410692b1e85b444c89de560d06d61b37
msgid ""
"The HS1 Trim value must match the Live stick centered Roll value and the "
"HS2 Trim value must match the live stick centered Pitch value or the "
"autopilot will think your stick is not centered when it is."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/troubleshooting.rst:256 f30462b0af01434ca56006a90c548e63
msgid "Make sure the CG (center of gravity) of the copter is dead center."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/troubleshooting.rst:257 c0abaad4278b4eb09254b24ebae8efc1
msgid ""
"Then run the level command on a flat surface. (hold disarm for ~15 "
"seconds to invoke.)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/troubleshooting.rst:260 5dcc90a3d57d4b5c822554fc02808475
msgid ""
"You can also fly in auto-trim mode in a windless (important!) environment"
"--any wind will cause the changes you make to work against you when the "
"quad rotates 180°."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/troubleshooting.rst:263 59059cc45dda479b9a0f9673589e399c
msgid "Hold arm for ~ 20 seconds to fly in auto-trim mode for about 45 seconds."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/troubleshooting.rst:265 ea6700ad55a842c0bade3658205f2c58
msgid ""
"It will exit auto-trim automatically and fly normally. (Don't worry about"
" counting out the time, just wait for the flashing lights to enter either"
" mode."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/troubleshooting.rst:269 66e0292e71334e2bbc792374ae1975e6
msgid ""
"Avoid it if possible but if you have to you can use some radio pitch and "
"roll trims"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/troubleshooting.rst:271 ce438857d95c41e68a6e9c98f0310563
msgid "NEVER use any radio trim for Yaw."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/troubleshooting.rst:274 87383eeb0a684dbf8cd0c9cc31d32425
msgid "The quad always wants to yaw to the right or left when I take off"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/troubleshooting.rst:276 57e3f98db28849be8672b3a121a2cadc
msgid ""
"This is usually due to an airframe that it out of tune, either with one "
"motor tilted slightly or the weight balance not centered. Typically, the "
"bad behavior is that the quad will always yaw by a certain amount, "
"typically around 30-45 degrees on takeoff, but will stay in that "
"direction. This is because the mechanical asymmetry of an out-of-tune "
"quad is forcing the yaw, and the limit is just how long it take for the P"
" term in the AC2's PID equations to grow and stop it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/troubleshooting.rst:284 7a0ddba4161342b4aabf88abb16abe7c
msgid ""
"Although you can adjust those terms in the Mission Planner's PID "
"configuration screen, the best solution is to solve it at the source. "
"Eyeball each motor and see if it or the arm it is one is slightly tilted,"
" and bend it back to vertical if so. Also ensure that the battery is "
"centered on the quad and the center of gravity of the vehicle is as close"
" to the center of the APM board as possible. It's also a great idea to "
"redo the ESC calibration routine just in case."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/troubleshooting.rst:292 76110aa88e03402289cae8b187e4aad6
msgid ""
"Also make sure that you've got your forward-rotating and counter-rotating"
" props on the right motors, as shown \\ :ref:`here <connect-escs-and-"
"motors_attach_propellers>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/troubleshooting.rst:297 c3b662e0ae4b425cba42ed1a04a45ea2
msgid "The quad always wants to yaw when I pitch or roll"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/troubleshooting.rst:299 17c9ba08bece4bae9c0a2a3467e372f6
msgid ""
"Your compass may need calibrating. AC2 calibrates while flying to take "
"the magnetic fields of your motors into account. You must disarm the "
"motors at the end of your flight to save your calibration to EEPROM. It "
"usually takes 1-2 flights before the calibration makes a difference."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/troubleshooting.rst:304 222efe291a634a4eb05d06b24c062210
msgid ""
"You must use a compass in conjunction with a GPS. If you disable your "
"compass, please disconnect your GPS or you will have random Yaw hold "
"issue."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/troubleshooting.rst:309 e07186ae11744db0ae36f8df8a51302a
msgid ""
"My copter flies well, but then dips a motor arm in a fidgety manner while"
" hovering"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/troubleshooting.rst:311 9aba0eda7b3e497c822a297467f02115
msgid ""
"The most likely cause of this is your bullet connectors. After your "
"initial setup, when you test motor direction and electrical connections, "
"we\\ **strongly** recommend that you cut off the bullet connectors and "
"directly solder the motors wires to the ESC wires. That way is vibration-"
"proof and will lead to much more reliable operation."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/troubleshooting.rst:317 a9d213ddd77f484e87aaef746b36c450
msgid ""
"If your wires are soldered without bullet connectors and you still see "
"this issue, there are some other things that can cause this problem. Your"
" motor may be going bad: either the bearings are going or your shaft is "
"horribly bent. A motor with bad bearings takes more power to spin. In "
"that situation, the ESC could be cutting out to protect itself. Or it "
"maybe flying slow enough to stall the motor. Attach a current sensor "
"between your battery and the motor and test the difference between a good"
" motor and the bad one. If you are seeing higher draw in the bad one, "
"replace or fix it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/troubleshooting.rst:328 bdb0e125082f4cdead44df041e50a20d
msgid ""
"Another possible cause is the THR_MIN parameter's default of 130 is too "
"low for your motors or ESCs. The THR_MIN parameter controls the minimum "
"throttle that will be sent to the ESCs/motors to ensure they keep "
"spinning while your throttle is above zero. If this parameter is too low "
"it is possible for your motors to momentarily stop leading to a twitch. "
"Try increasing this parameter to 150. You should be careful not to set "
"this parameter too high (i.e. >150 especially on a high powered copter)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/troubleshooting.rst:340 e7d2b25ba34a4757bb6ce972712e7be6
msgid "One of my motors started shaking and then burned out!"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/troubleshooting.rst:342 667ccc01011c42d4aa414b2980526f3c
msgid ""
"Motors will typically twitch once per second or so (often with a beep) "
"while they're waiting for a signal. That's normal. But sometimes, if you "
"have not followed the warnings and disconnected the four-wire signal "
"cable, they may vibrate rapidly when you reset your board. This is a rare"
" problem that can crop up with certain ESC types that don't handle out-"
"of-range signals well (it's a problem with the Arduino bootloader during "
"bootup, so there's not much we can do about it). But it can always be "
"avoided by following the advice of this warning, which is liberally "
"sprinkled throughout the manual!"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/troubleshooting.rst:353 4b586ed8cf9e45f8ae6d2ef20ffc600f
msgid ""
"The APM board works when it's plugged into the USB, but not when it's "
"powered by the RC rail (ESC/Lipo)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/troubleshooting.rst:355 7281667a7e5f4d3e95348c02352a5490
msgid ""
"APM allows for both RC power or a separate battery run through APM's "
"built-in power regulator as described \\ :ref:`here <common-"
"apm25-and-26-overview>`. A solder jumper called SJ1 determines which is "
"used. By default from the factory, that jumper should be soldered which "
"means APM will be powered by the RC rail."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/troubleshooting.rst:362 6c23375e5ddd4c1197f7c50015db610c
msgid ""
"If for some reason, connecting power to the RC rail does not power your "
"board, check to see if that jumper is soldered. If it isn't, as shown "
"below, just solder a blob of solder over the two pads to connect them."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/troubleshooting.rst:370 da8301a54aa74b0a82cd88a1590b6b68
msgid ""
"In the \"raw sensor view\" of the Mission Planner, the Z accelerometer "
"reading is always 1000, not 0, when it's on the bench"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/troubleshooting.rst:372 08de4733da094d63b46880608d17918d
msgid ""
"That's normal. The Z accelerometer is showing the force of gravity (which"
" is an acceleration, as you'll recall from high school physics). If you "
"turn the quad upside down, you'll see that it reverses."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/troubleshooting.rst:377 c82c569725714e3d829168be72bb2d34
msgid "I can't connect with the Mission Planner over Xbee"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/troubleshooting.rst:379 8b56556ffc984a6f8f4286d110d2b246
msgid ""
"First, remember that you cannot use wireless telemetry with APM 2 while "
"the USB cable is plugged in (they use the same port). Make sure it's "
"unplugged."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/troubleshooting.rst:383 9ee94c5fa96549f8ae64827b713cf162
msgid ""
"Check that you've switching the Mission Planner to the COM port assigned "
"to your Xbee that's connected to your PC and set the baud rate to 57600."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/troubleshooting.rst:386 5fd472c07a9b48cca21516fb51f476a1
msgid ""
"Ensure that you've gone through the full Xbee configuration process "
"described \\ :ref:`here. <common-telemetry-xbee>` Run the test code at "
"the bottom of that page to ensure that your Xbees are communicating. \\ "
"**Remember that the Xbees must be programmed in X-CTU to run at 57k.**"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/troubleshooting.rst:392 5df58b698098487cab9238c3838705eb
msgid ""
"If your Xbees suddenly stop working, it could be that a loose cable has "
"led to a signal glitch that bricked the airborne one."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/troubleshooting.rst:396 ec767b1652a34c57b4cbe50bb35a96f5
msgid "I can't connect with the Mission Planner with the 3DR Radios"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/troubleshooting.rst:398 a015c0576a914598a42c9561e9146e1b
msgid ""
"Remember that you cannot use wireless telemetry with APM 2 while the USB "
"cable is plugged in (they use the same port). Make sure it's unplugged."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/troubleshooting.rst:401 0af081aeaa124718aa64c6cea70d82af
msgid ""
"Check that you've switching the Mission Planner to the COM port assigned "
"to your 3DR radio that's connected to your PC and set the baud rate to "
"57600."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/troubleshooting.rst:406 d5e5ff30bdfa441b9a721aefea013a6b
msgid "I'm having trouble connecting via MAVLink over USB"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/troubleshooting.rst:408 61ac5ee1d1544a8e8f8e65f19f14b2d8
msgid ""
"Double check that you've selected the right COM port and baud rate for "
"USB (115200). Also double check in your Windows Device Manager which COM "
"port your APM has been assigned to. It will be listed under Ports."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/troubleshooting.rst:412 f9d21edb998e47d18af0914e4227d10f
msgid ""
"Did you use the MP installation program, which installs the drivers, and "
"did it complete successfully?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/troubleshooting.rst:414 26bda32985bc4e6785fa5852a2e00497
msgid "Have you loaded flight firmware (Copter or Plane) to APM?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/troubleshooting.rst:415 fb3707e375484333b119e4436d0a45f9
msgid "Did you press Connect in the MP?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/troubleshooting.rst:418 166b5d51ed2942eca4e9d1ee02cf5fe9
msgid "Copter ESCs keep beeping"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/troubleshooting.rst:420 45c73731bc404361bea8d4281e30b228
msgid "Have you set up your RC input in the Mission Planner setup process?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/troubleshooting.rst:421 2281d46ed3c74ad8b25e81fe55b38523
msgid ""
"Check that your RC radio is talking properly to its receiver (typically, "
"that will be shown by a green light on the receiver)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/troubleshooting.rst:423 f5a4112c90964726b08a8eb048295697
msgid ""
"If those two check out, you probably just need to calibrate your ESCs "
"(even if you've done that before, if you've erased your EEPROM and done a"
" reset, you'll need to do it again. It's an easy procedure and the "
"instructions are \\ :ref:`here. <esc-calibration>`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/troubleshooting.rst:428 0fed6dd7ed2b44a0bb141ef72f827e7a
msgid ""
"Check that your radio channels are in right order (cabling and/or Mode-1,"
" Mode-2)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/troubleshooting.rst:438 2eaca299f41d4e5e85a7ff5aa514f843
msgid ""
"I'm using a MediaTek GPS, and although the module's blue lock LED goes "
"solid, APM is not showing a lock or I'm getting \"No GPS\" on the Mission"
" Planner display"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/troubleshooting.rst:440 1d3ff2272aab46f28c7c8f8e05573f0d
msgid "I'm also not getting GPS data in my telemetry."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/troubleshooting.rst:442 c360dc3d76444517af0dd1862022be91
msgid "Solution:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/troubleshooting.rst:444 1ec8edcd72d140ed88afc6e1aa96e632
msgid ""
"There are a few things that could cause this. First, make sure that your "
"input voltage to APM is not too high. It should be in the range 5-5.4v. "
"\\ **Do not power the board at 6v.**"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/troubleshooting.rst:448 ee7fb188d6b04746a9f3f8d55d4f8ccd
msgid ""
"Second, ensure that you've selected the right MediaTek version in the "
"config file. If you bought your module from the DIY Drones store before "
"December, 2010, it had version 1.0 firmware: select GPS_PROTOCOL_MTK in "
"the config file. If you bought it after that, it as version 1.6 firmware:"
" select GPS_PROTOCOL_MTK16 in the config file."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/troubleshooting.rst:454 5df799d1a79d456081e19848812a6dbc
msgid ""
"Finally, if things still aren't working, check your cable. Cables longer "
"than the short one that ships with the GPS module tend to have thinner "
"wires that can break."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/troubleshooting.rst:459 079c394311a847a185bb74a125d7d89f
msgid "I'm not getting GPS lock at all"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/troubleshooting.rst:461 cd7ea1abd5ab491180618b294a213f1f
msgid ""
"First, note that you will probably only get GPS lock outside (if you're "
"very near a window or are lucky enough to have strong GPS signal and a "
"radio-transparent roof, you may be able to get lock inside). For a cold "
"start (power on), it make take three minutes or more. If you're just "
"hitting the reset button after lock has been achieved, it should take "
"less than a minute."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/troubleshooting.rst:468 04b099e097d542a8869799b488c8db74
msgid ""
"If you can't get GPS lock outside after many minutes(the blue LED on the "
"MediaTek module keeps blinking), you may have a defective module. If the "
"module shows lock, but the red APM GPS LED is still blinking, you either "
"have a bad cable or an older MediaTek module that needs to have its "
"firmware updated."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/troubleshooting.rst:474 b49c812db9d64075a9fb5a776366a9da
msgid ""
"If you have a uBlox module and APM is not showing lock, you may not have "
"ordered the module preprogrammed for ArduPilot from the DIY Drones store "
"(you had to select that option). In that case, program it yourself with "
"the instructions `here <https://diydrones.com/profiles/blogs/tutorial-"
"programming-your>`__."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/troubleshooting.rst:481 dbfd33d0b82742288d0d12a6954e1e1e
msgid "I want to load the code in Arduino, but I'm having trouble"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/troubleshooting.rst:483 00bfd574c4194b54be5fb519b7ae7fe0
msgid ""
"First, ensure you've downloaded the latest AC2 code and are using the "
"latest Arduino (at least 022)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/troubleshooting.rst:486 af6637aa1bda473c85599fa6458dbbd3
msgid ""
"If you're getting compile errors, that means that you haven't told "
"Arduino where your libraries are properly."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/troubleshooting.rst:488 06507284357f4651a1271d5e86ef237d
msgid ""
"If you're getting avrdude sync error message in the Arduino status bar), "
"go through *all* of the tips `here <https://diydrones.com/profiles/blogs"
"/arduino-debugging-tips>`__."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/troubleshooting.rst:492 479dacc796f84630a48fa999a2633f17
msgid "My Copter just won't rise/lift off"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/troubleshooting.rst:494 a79c7fd216f94363a9c57a8dcbad31ad
msgid ""
"If you have a problems on getting your Copter up from the ground, check "
"that you propellers are mounted correct way."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/troubleshooting.rst:497 690a9218ae96450e86540293182b7453
msgid ""
"Official Copter propellers are not symmetric and they need to be mounted "
"proper way. If you look close to center mounting hole, there are small "
"texts saying 10x45 or 12x45. There texts needs to be upside."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/troubleshooting.rst:501 11dc03e35f124853800993666aedb309
msgid "If text is on downside, your Copter cannot lift properly."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/troubleshooting.rst:503 80e105e7b9804507a66d362687428400
msgid ""
"The props that came with early version of the 3D Robotics Copter were "
"also of uneven quality and some would flex too much under load, reducing "
"lift. If you have these (black and more flexible than most props), you "
"should replace them with APC or equivalent props or contact 3D Robotics "
"for a replacement."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/troubleshooting.rst:510 660700fb201a4acfa444b2ae818eca8d
msgid "My Copter feels sloppy on roll or pitch axis"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/troubleshooting.rst:512 86d138feb613461abed1cabb6c11ca8c
msgid ""
"Both of roll and pitch axis should give to you exact or close to similar "
"response when doing hand tests. If one of the axis does not respond as "
"expected, check your PID settings and you can try to upload firmware "
"again."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/troubleshooting.rst:517 07fc38e27cf54f5c87f1206e1d25e15b
msgid ""
"Firmware upload might be successful but due internal timing issues some "
"of the code is not written properly and this can cause unstableness on "
"your Copter."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/troubleshooting.rst:522 c05e61f954a74f7ab90eaa6325b218f3
msgid "I've got a tricopter and I'm having trouble with the yaw"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/troubleshooting.rst:524 2f9f2350b2c14b66aa41387466046a98
msgid ""
"Please see the tips `here <https://diydrones.com/profiles/blogs"
"/tricopter-based-on-arducopter-yaw-issues-and-solutions>`__."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/troubleshooting.rst:527 58cb5bc7be38452e98d6eca2139845d0
msgid ""
"The Mission Planner's HUD display moves around even though my copter is "
"motionless"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/troubleshooting.rst:529 7a6417af1073434e84b4c55ae73855b6
msgid ""
"As of 2.9 we've enabled a feature that uses GPS velocities/accelerations "
"to compensate for centrifugal forces. It should not cause you any issues "
"in flight but you can disable it by setting the AHRS_GPS_GAIN to 0 "
"through the Mission Planner's Advanced Parameters List if it makes you "
"uncomfortable. \\ `Here is a video "
"<https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SXppOmZ3Rpg&feature=player_detailpage>`__"
" demonstrating the problem and the explanation as to why it happens."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/troubleshooting.rst:537 b6b977fca4504cf09dbedde96fa6b365
msgid ""
"My copter's is moving a lot on the Mission Planner map even though it's "
"sitting still in my house"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/troubleshooting.rst:539 dc34df779f6443a5983a09f00471b437
msgid ""
"a GPS will rarely give an accurate position while indoors or near walls "
"due to multipath-ing. If you go outside you should see that it settles "
"down."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/troubleshooting.rst:547 4346592105e44d5898a6f548039b9ced
msgid "In Alt-Hold or loiter my copter is very bouncy or takes off into the sky"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/troubleshooting.rst:549 c8efae710f054aadb77d8e5d4ec046b8
msgid ""
"Copter uses the accelerometers heavily for calculating altitude and climb"
" rate. Vibrations from the motors can cause the accelerometer values to "
"become very noisy and throw off the altitude estimates. The solution is "
"to reduce sources of vibration from your frame (bent prop adapters and "
"motor shafts, replace EPP props with APC) and remove hard connections "
"between the frame and APM by using vibration dampening products such as "
"\\ `DuBro foam <https://www.dubro.com/products/r-c-protective-foam-"
"rubber>`__."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/troubleshooting.rst:557 6a473c1e9b1841bdaa9418cf3cd68d3f
msgid ""
"As of version 2.9 of Copter, inertial navigation is used to maintain "
"altitude and in 2.9.2 horizontal position as well so vibration truly "
"needs to be minimized. A small pad (1/2 to 1\") of Kyosho Zeal tape under"
" each corner of the autopilot should get vibration low enough."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/troubleshooting.rst:562 7d291f3c0bda47a89e5e686b22e57dc3
msgid ""
"Or you can use a standoff with an extending screw 1/10\" outboard of each"
" corner of the autopilot and suspend it with four 1/16\" O-rings of "
"appropriate diameter such that when folded over through the autopilot "
"they just hold the board without movement, but also not in much tension."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/troubleshooting.rst:568 c9d72d7c6cf34234a2e4b5925c5678f6
msgid ""
"When a flight log is checked in RAW mode, X,Y and Z hovering ACCELs "
"vibration on a log graph should at least be in the + to - 5 range and "
"either of the above techniques can get them down to the plus to minus 1 "
"range if they are just right."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/troubleshooting.rst:573 b90fc813695947b3817532022be764b0
msgid ""
"You can graph your Z-axis vibrations by enabling the \"RAW\" message type"
" in the :ref:`dataflash <common-downloading-and-analyzing-data-logs-in-"
"mission-planner>`, then take a short flight in Stabilize mode and finally"
" download the logs and graph them with the mission planner. Below is an "
"example of an extremely badly vibrating copter (top) and a nearly "
"perfectly smooth copter (bottom). The Z-axis vibrations should not move "
"up and down more than -5 to -15 or alt hold will not function correctly."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/troubleshooting.rst:584 0ac2b8ab6b18434ab14171dae01abf14
msgid ""
"There is a good discussion `here <https://diydrones.com/forum/topics"
"/vibration-isolation-and-dampening-of-apm-px4-for-version-2-9>`__ re "
"vibration dampening."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/troubleshooting.rst:587 6d623393abf044ea9497cc3f0f4322a5
msgid "My copter slowly rises or descends when I enable Alt-Hold or Loiter"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/troubleshooting.rst:589 086717a6348b4a9d9cad9fd7a1328c91
#, python-format
msgid ""
"The throttle deadband when in alt-hold or loiter mode is from 40% ~ 60% "
"of the throttle. If you have an overpowered or underpowered copter then "
"when in stabilize mode the throttle required to maintain a hover may be "
"outside this range. So when you engage alt-hold or loiter your throttle "
"is outside of the deadband and Copter thinks you want to climb or "
"descend. The simple solution is to move your throttle back to centre as "
"you engage alt-hold. A permanent solution is to modify the THR_MID "
"parameter through the mission planner's Adv Parameter List screen. If "
"your copter hover at 40% of throttle stick when in stabilize mode, make "
"THR_MID = 400. If it hovers at 60% of throttle make the parameter 600, "
"etc."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/troubleshooting.rst:602 efffc623609b4e1aaacb297daee5e668
msgid "Mission planner displays enormous \"Distance to Home\" or \"Dist to WP\""
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/troubleshooting.rst:604 87cc869256ae403e8e2e7a6fe38605c2
msgid ""
"The \"Dist to WP\" (distance to waypoint) default to -1 when you do not "
"have a waypoint set. Because of a display issue the mission planner "
"displays this negative number as 65535. It will display a proper number "
"as soon as you enter loiter mode, guided more or start a mission. This "
"display issue will be fixed in a future version of Copter."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/troubleshooting.rst:610 1c47dd993a654935823b67483cd2cf0b
msgid ""
"The \"Distance to Home\" is the distance from the mission planner's home "
"location (which is different from the Copter's home location which is set"
" when you arm the motors). You can reset the mission planner's home "
"location to the copter's current location by clicking on the \"Home "
"Location\" link in the Flight Planner screen. Below is a video "
"explanation and how resolve it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/troubleshooting.rst:618 c8ac6b996ba24709bbbb33eb9c91dab9
msgid ""
"Mission Planner displays Altitude of -21 million or NaN appearing in "
"Advanced Parameter list"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/troubleshooting.rst:620 3d38141be30842c488a43edfe2c52626
msgid ""
"You should not attempt to fly your copter in this state because "
":ref:`AltHold <altholdmode>` will not function at all. You should instead"
" go into the Mission Planner's Terminal screen and reset your parameters "
"by typing \"setup\", \"reset\", \"Y\" and rebooting your APM."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/troubleshooting.rst:626 dca142104457436280fbcd87fd2ded5c
msgid ""
"The cause is a bug in the 2.9.1 accelerometer calibration routine that "
"can save NaN to the AHRS AHRS Trim parameters if the calibration routine "
"fails. This feeds into the inertial navigation routines and results in "
"the bizarre altitude display in the mission planner. Note: you will "
"likely have seen \"Calibration failed (????)\" after you attempted to "
"calibrate the accelerometers values."
msgstr ""

